-**Parameters and Edge Cases:**
-- **Bi-linear Interpolation:** Assumes a linear function along the ray within a cell, meaning no extremal value exists strictly inside the cell [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Precision:** Floating-point precision in interpolation may affect accuracy in thin structures [S1].
-- **Empty Regions:** The min/max grid effectively skips empty regions if their max value is below the current threshold [S1].
+**Parameters:**
+*   `MAX_NUM_STEPS`: In some implementations (e.g., UnityVolumeRendering), a maximum number of steps for ray marching is defined, e.g., 512 [S3, unofficial]. This may act as a safeguard against infinite loops or excessive computation in degenerate cases.
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Precision:** Bi-linear interpolation assumes linearity. If the volume data has high-frequency details within a cell, the maximum might be missed [S1].
+*   **Boundaries:** Correctly handling ray entry/exit from the volume and leaf nodes is critical.
+*   **Degenerate Input:** Empty volumes or volumes with uniform values should be handled gracefully (e.g., returning the uniform value).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Depends on the distribution of high-intensity values. In the best case (high-intensity structure near the front), it is fast due to early termination. In the worst case, it may traverse many nodes.
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the min/max grid and the priority queue. The grid size is proportional to the volume resolution, and the queue size depends on the branching factor and traversal depth.
-- **Strengths:** Efficient for visualizing thin structures like blood vessels or contrast-enhanced tissues by skipping low-intensity regions [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Assumes linear interpolation within cells, which may miss extrema inside cells [S1][S2, unofficial]. Performance degrades if high-intensity values are distributed throughout the volume.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Best case $O(1)$ if the maximum is found in the root. Worst case $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, if the maximum is in a leaf that requires full traversal. Average case is significantly faster than standard ray marching due to pruning [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$ where $M$ is the number of nodes in the min/max grid. The priority queue size is bounded by the number of nodes [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Highly efficient for sparse data or data with distinct high-intensity structures (e.g., blood vessels) [S1]. Skips large portions of the volume.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of the min/max grid. Less effective for dense, uniform data where pruning is ineffective.
+*   **When to use:** Medical imaging, visualization of thin structures [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When the volume is dense and lacks high-intensity outliers, or when memory is constrained and the grid overhead is too high.
 
 ## Background
-This technique was suggested by Parker et al. in [PPL* 99] and is mainly used in real-world medical imaging applications [S1]. It is part of the broader field of interactive volume rendering with ray tracing [S1].
+The method was suggested by Parker et al. in [PPL* 99] as an important technique for real-world medical imaging applications [S1]. It uses a priority queue in conjunction with a spatial data structure, specifically min/max multilevel grids, to track cells with maximum values [S1]. While Parker et al. used min/max multilevel grids, other common min/max hierarchical data structures could also be exploited [S1]. The technique is part of the broader field of ray tracing-based volume rendering, which has been developed since the early 1990s [S1].
 
